<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mediterranean</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mediterranean Sea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mediterranean Sea lies between southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. It has been one of the great arenas of trade, migration and warfare throughout history. Empires such as the Punic, Roman, Byzantine, Fatimid and Ottoman empires competed to control its routes, while trans-Saharan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> networks linked it to distant regions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mediterranean.jpg" alt="Mediterranean" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: of the modification : Eric Gaba (Sting) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Mediterranean Sea is an intercontinental sea between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, almost completely enclosed by land. It connects to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a>, only 14 km wide, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> by the Bosporus, and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>. It covers about 2,500,000 km2 and has fifteen marginal seas, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, Adriatic, Tyrrhenian and Marmara. Its average depth is 1,500 m, and the deepest point, 5,109 m, lies in the Calypso Deep in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> is the longest river to end in it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> are its two largest islands.</p><p>Geological evidence indicates that around 5.9 million years ago the sea was cut off from the Atlantic and was partly or completely dried up over some 600,000 years, in the Messinian salinity crisis. It was refilled about 5.3 million years ago by the Zanclean flood, in less than two years according to scientists' estimates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, in the eastern Mediterranean, was among the first regions in the world with permanent human habitation, as early as 12,000 BCE. The earliest advanced civilisations on the sea were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoans</a>, who traded extensively with each other. Around 1200 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Bronze Age Collapse</a> destroyed many cities and trade routes in the east. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> later colonised its coastlines extensively. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, built a canal linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and so to the Mediterranean, wide enough for two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> to pass and needing four days to traverse.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> of the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> and became the preeminent power in the western Mediterranean. Once Augustus founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, the Romans called it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mare-nostrum">Mare Nostrum</a>, Our Sea. For the next 400 years the empire controlled the sea and virtually all its coasts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, which earned it the nickname Roman Lake. Rome is the only state to have ever controlled all of its coasts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> also called the sea Mare Magnum and Mare Internum. The name Mare Mediterraneum, meaning in the middle of land, is first attested in the 6th century in the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a>. Arabic writers called it the Sea of the Romans, and the Ancient Egyptians called it Wadj-wr, great green. The countries on its shores include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> are island countries within it. Its mild winters and warm summers give their name to the Mediterranean climate type.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mediterranean Sea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Baghdad sat where transcontinental trade routes linking the <b>Mediterranean</b>, Central Asia, the Indian subcontinent and Tang China met, and at its peak it was the commercial hub of the known world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade networks connected Persia to the Indian subcontinent, Central Asia and the <b>Mediterranean</b>, and a canal completed under Darius linked the Nile to the Red Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the second century olive oil became a major export, carried in African amphorae, and the fine red tableware now called African Red Slip ware was traded across the <b>Mediterranean</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Slaves were obtained through the trans-Saharan trade, through <b>Mediterranean</b> commerce and from raids on Sicily and Italy; 5,000 black Zanj slaves are recorded as part of the garrison of al-Abbasiyya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He then campaigned into Elam to the east and up the Euphrates into Syria, reaching the Cedar Forest and the Silver Mountains towards the <b>Mediterranean</b>, while later legend carried his armies into Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Gold, silver and bronze issues, inscribed mainly in Greek and later also in Ge'ez, enabled Aksum to participate fully in the <b>Mediterranean</b> monetary economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
